sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/aristotle-pa02-29> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Birds have no ears but only the auditory passages, because their skin is hard and they have feathers instead of hair; the same is true of oviparous quadrupeds clad in scaly plates.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Birds and scaly oviparous quadrupeds have no external ears";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Aristotle, On the Parts of Animals (De Partibus Animalium), Book II, Part 12; translated by William Ogle, The Works of Aristotle Translated into English, ed. J. A. Smith and W. D. Ross, Oxford: Clarendon Press, vol. V (1912). Scope: birds and scaly oviparous quadrupeds (reptiles)"
}
